(A function reads a top-level timer as a timer, written above the declaration or below it)
To 'report the race clock'.
    a number called waited is the 'race clock''s elapsed in milliseconds.
    If waited is greater than or equal to 40 then, Print "above: at least 40 milliseconds".
    Otherwise, Print "above: only {waited} milliseconds".
    If the 'race clock''s elapsed in seconds is less than 1000 then, Print "above: under a thousand seconds".
    Print "above: running {the 'race clock''s running}".

a timer called 'race clock'.

To 'start the race clock'.
    Start the 'race clock'.

To 'stop the race clock'.
    Stop the 'race clock'.

(A timer declared inside a function is its own, and shadows nothing it should not)
To 'time a lap of its own'.
    a timer called lap.
    Start the lap.
    Wait 20 milliseconds.
    Stop the lap.
    a number called taken is the lap's duration in milliseconds.
    If taken is greater than or equal to 20 then, Print "own lap: at least 20 milliseconds".
    Otherwise, Print "own lap: only {taken} milliseconds".

'start the race clock'.
Wait 50 milliseconds.
'report the race clock'.
'report the race clock'.
'stop the race clock'.
Print "running after the stop: {the 'race clock''s running}".
a number called total is the 'race clock''s duration in milliseconds.
If total is greater than or equal to 50 then, Print "top: duration at least 50 milliseconds".
If the 'race clock''s start time is greater than 0 then, Print "top: start time set".
If the 'race clock''s end time is greater than or equal to the 'race clock''s start time then, Print "top: end time set".

(A timer declared in every branch is a global too)
a boolean called 'the race is long' is true.
If 'the race is long' then,
    a timer called lap.
Otherwise,
    a timer called lap.

To 'read the lap'.
    Print "lap running: {the lap's running}".

Start the lap.
'read the lap'.
'time a lap of its own'.
'read the lap'.

(A timer declared in a loop body keeps to the loop)
a number called round is 0.
While round is less than 2,
    a timer called split,
    Start the split,
    Print "split running: {the split's running}",
    increment round.
